          "Kyle's lambda regression thus quantifies adverse selection directly by measuring covariance between net signed volume and realized price changes. Intraday monitoring also reveals predictable liquidity U-curves: `lambda` typically drops tightly around high-volume open and close auctions while widening sharply across thin midday sessions or right ahead of macro releases. Comparing `KylesLambdaModel` impact estimates across deep versus thin books (`lambda: 3e-05` projecting `USD 0.15` vs `lambda: 0.0009` projecting `USD 4.5` for `5k` shares) highlights why static commission schedules mislead execution algorithms. When liquidity dries up ahead of Federal Reserve announcements or quarterly earnings calls, linear lambda increases thirty-fold within minutes even while quoted midpoint prices barely budge. Quantitative desks re-fit regression slopes over rolling 15-minute consolidated tape buckets, guard degenerate zero-volume intervals defensively (`volume <= 0` returning safe fallback estimates rather than `ZeroDivisionError`), and feed live `estimate_price_impact` sensitivity curves straight into smart parent-order slicing horizons before committing institutional blocks into fragile midday books. Remember too that Kyle originally modeled batch auction clearing where anonymous uninformed noise orders camouflage informed institutional imbalances: in modern continuous limit order books, time-of-day seasonalities strongly skew lambda upward right at open (`09:30–09:45`) and down toward closing liquidity pools (`15:45–16:00`). Validating linear scaling across varying share blocks confirms clean proportional behavior while warning execution desks whenever order sizes pass small-order linear approximations into deeper book tiers where marginal fill impact steepens sharply. Historical calibration logs confirm how linear impact sensitivity scales smoothly across small-cap versus mega-cap liquidity cohorts, letting institutional desk routers avoid stale impact coefficients across heterogeneous execution universes.",
          "One critical modeling boundary separates single-slice Kyle's lambda from multi-day parent execution: linear `lambda * Q` fits individual aggressive slices well inside stationary book depths, whereas splitting massive multi-percent-ADV parent orders across hours bends into concave sub-linear curves that we model next with the Square-Root Law."
