        "example": "A thermostat that continuously adjusts heating power based on recent temperature changes, giving more weight to the last 5 minutes than this morning.",
        "code": "class RecursiveLeastSquaresLambda:\n    def __init__(self, forgetting_factor=0.98):\n        self.lam_decay = forgetting_factor\n        self.theta = 0.0001 # Estimated slope (Kyle's lambda)\n        self.p_cov = 1.0     # Estimation covariance\n\n    def update(self, signed_volume, delta_price):\n        # RLS update recursions for scalar parameter\n        x = signed_volume\n        y = delta_price\n        \n        # 1. Compute prediction error\n        error = y - (self.theta * x)\n        \n        # 2. Compute Kalman gain\n        k_gain = (self.p_cov * x) / (self.lam_decay + self.p_cov * x * x)\n        \n        # 3. Update parameter estimate\n        self.theta += k_gain * error\n        \n        # 4. Update covariance\n        self.p_cov = (self.p_cov - k_gain * x * self.p_cov) / self.lam_decay\n        return round(self.theta, 6)\n\nrls = RecursiveLeastSquaresLambda(forgetting_factor=0.95)\n# Stream 4 trades with increasing price impact\ntrades = [(1000, 0.05), (1500, 0.12), (2000, 0.18), (3000, 0.35)]\nfor q, dp in trades:\n    est_lambda = rls.update(q, dp)\n    print(f\"trade Q={q}, dP={dp} -> updated Kyle's lambda = {est_lambda}\")",
