        "title": "Avellaneda-Stoikov Market Making — Core Algorithm & State Representation",
        "say": [
          "Beyond calculating the asymmetric reservation price `r`, the Avellaneda-Stoikov framework derives the exact closed-form total bid-ask spread that maximizes expected terminal wealth net of inventory variance. Pinpointing reservation center `r` establishes directional inventory bias; deriving optimal spread width balances fill frequency against profit per filled share.",
          "Let order book liquidity density be modeled as an exponential fill-intensity decay function parameterized by `kappa` (`lambda(delta) = A * exp(-kappa * delta)`). Large `kappa` characterizes competitive, price-sensitive books where stepping one tick wider causes fill arrivals to collapse rapidly; small `kappa` characterizes captive, thin order books where wider quotes still capture steady passive fills.",
          "Inside `OptimalQuotesEngine.calculate_quotes(mid_price, inventory_q, gamma=0.1, volatility=0.02, kappa=1.5, t_rem=1.0)`, total optimal spread combines two additive terms: `term1 = gamma * (var) * t_rem` plus `term2 = (2.0 / gamma) * math.log(1.0 + (gamma / kappa))`. Splitting `total_spread / 2.0` symmetrically above and below reservation price `r` yields `bid = r - half_spread` and `ask = r + half_spread`.",
          "Inspect the non-obvious mathematical invariant inside `term2` as risk aversion shrinks (`gamma -> 0`): by first-order Taylor expansion (`ln(1 + x) ~= x`), `(2 / gamma) * ln(1 + gamma / kappa)` converges smoothly to `2 / kappa`. Even a risk-neutral market maker quotes a strictly positive minimum spread `2 / kappa` dictated purely by order-arrival elasticity.",
          "Notice also how inventory `q` moves quotes cleanly without changing total spread inside this constant-`kappa` approximation. Computing `bid_dist_from_mid = mid_price - bid` and `ask_dist_from_mid = ask - mid_price` lets execution diagnostics verify quote asymmetry around current midpoint while confirming `ask - bid` remains constant at `total_spread`.",
          "Walking through both simulation runs around `mid_price=100.0` (`gamma=0.1`, `vol=0.02`, `kappa=1.5`, `t_rem=1.0`) verifies exact floating-point arithmetic: `term1 = 0.00004` and `term2 ~= 1.2908`, putting `half_spread ~= 0.6454`. Flat inventory (`q=0`) rounds cleanly to symmetric `Bid=99.35 / Ask=100.65` (`0.65` distance each side); long `q=+50` lowers `r` to `99.998`, pulling `Ask` down to `100.64` (`ask_dist=0.64`) while holding `Bid` at `99.35`.",
          "Rounding `bid` and `ask` independently to two decimal cents inside `calculate_quotes` produces one tiny arithmetic nuance visible in the long case: unrounded spread equals `1.2908`, whereas rounded `100.64 - 99.35` reports `1.29` compared to `100.65 - 99.35 = 1.30` at neutral. Production tick aligners explicitly enforce exchange tick rules after rounding so quoted spreads never silently lose pennies.",
          "When holding long inventory, the market maker thus lowers both reservation center `r` and absolute quotes subtly to accelerate excess inventory unloading on the ask side while demanding deeper discounts before buying even more shares on the bid. Evaluating negative `inventory_q=-50` demonstrates exact mirror symmetry, tightening `bid_dist_from_mid` to prioritize short covering. Examining spread symmetry around `OptimalQuotesEngine.calculate_quotes` clarifies why long inventory adjusts midpoint location first before tilting bid-ask offsets (`q=0: Bid=USD 99.35 | Ask=USD 100.65`, `q=+50: Bid=USD 99.35 | Ask=USD 100.64`). Narrowing ask distance incentivizes incoming market buyers to lift excess shares passively while widening bid distance slows further unwanted long accumulation. In multi-asset quoting books, shared factor-variance adjustments extend single-stock Stoikov offsets cleanly across correlated ETF baskets—so holding overweight semiconductor chips automatically shades quotes in correlated leveraged funds simultaneously! Tick-grid validators also assert strict market-microstructure invariants before dispatch: resulting bid and ask quotes must preserve positive non-crossed spacing (`bid < ask`), round coherently onto valid exchange penny or nickel increments, incorporate order-book liquidity intensity `k` cleanly without division-by-zero risks (`k <= 0`), and guarantee quoted half-spreads clear exchange clearing fees plus rebates cleanly across every active ticker tier. Explicit exchange clearing-fee buffers further guarantee calculated bid-ask half-spreads never collapse beneath maker rebate break-even economics across high-turnover equities. Tracking inventory skew parameters alongside minimum half-spread floors prevents rebate-sensitive liquidity engines from quoting inside exchange clearing fees during hyper-quiet lunchtime regimes. Automated unit checks confirm half-spread bounds scale cleanly with tick increments across diverse equity price tiers.",
          "Practical multi-asset market makers estimate `kappa` empirically by regressing log fill-arrival rates against tick distance from best touch across rolling volume windows, updating `term2` slowly every few minutes while letting `r` react at microsecond cadence to live inventory fills and midpoint jitter."
        ],
        "example": "A currency exchange kiosk with too many Euros: they lower both their buying and selling rates for Euros, making it cheap for tourists to buy Euros while discouraging people from selling Euros to them.",
        "code": "import math\n\nclass OptimalQuotesEngine:\n    @classmethod\n    def calculate_quotes(cls, mid_price, inventory_q, gamma=0.1, volatility=0.02, kappa=1.5, t_rem=1.0):\n        # 1. Reservation price\n        var = volatility ** 2\n        r = mid_price - (inventory_q * gamma * var * t_rem)\n        \n        # 2. Optimal total spread\n        term1 = gamma * var * t_rem\n        term2 = (2.0 / gamma) * math.log(1.0 + (gamma / kappa))\n        total_spread = term1 + term2\n        half_spread = total_spread / 2.0\n        \n        # 3. Quotes centered around reservation price\n        bid = round(r - half_spread, 2)\n        ask = round(r + half_spread, 2)\n        return {\n            \"inventory\": inventory_q,\n            \"reservation_price\": round(r, 4),\n            \"optimal_bid\": bid,\n            \"optimal_ask\": ask,\n            \"spread\": round(ask - bid, 2),\n            \"bid_dist_from_mid\": round(mid_price - bid, 2),\n            \"ask_dist_from_mid\": round(ask - mid_price, 2)\n        }\n\nq_neutral = OptimalQuotesEngine.calculate_quotes(100.0, 0)\nq_long = OptimalQuotesEngine.calculate_quotes(100.0, 50)\n\nprint(\"neutral quotes (q=0): Bid=$\", q_neutral[\"optimal_bid\"], \"| Ask=$\", q_neutral[\"optimal_ask\"], f\"(symmetric dist: {q_neutral['bid_dist_from_mid']})\")\nprint(\"long quotes (q=+50): Bid=$\", q_long[\"optimal_bid\"], \"| Ask=$\", q_long[\"optimal_ask\"], f\"(bid dist={q_long['bid_dist_from_mid']}, ask dist={q_long['ask_dist_from_mid']})\")",
