          "Meanwhile, the two-scale realized volatility estimator resolves microstructure bid-ask bounce noise across tick intervals so desks detect whether volatile sweep bursts are shifting `avg_trade_size` and `cancel_rate_pct` intraday. Note a subtle input check for production wrappers: clamping `cancel_rate_pct` inside `[0.0, 1.0]` ensures malformed percentages above `100%` never turn `effective_queue` negative. Evaluating Poisson arrival decay through `FillHazardRateModel.calculate_fill_probability` quantifies exact execution odds across queue depths (`500 ahead: 86.6%` versus `5,000 ahead: 23.7%`) once expected cancellation attrition discounts stale resting orders ahead of us. Clamping malformed cancellation percentages strictly within `[0.0, 1.0]` prevents negative effective queue depth calculations from exploding exponential survival expressions! Notice the dual dynamics inside survival probability estimation: queue depletion accelerates both when incoming market orders consume top-of-book liquidity and when nervous high-frequency rivals cancel resting quotes ahead of us, though sudden rival cancellations right ahead of price jumps often indicate imminent informed flow rather than harmless windfalls! Quant execution researchers validate model calibration empirically by binning historical exchange limit placements against realized fill frequencies across spread widths and volatility regimes, verifying monotonic probability decay as queue depth widens and asserting safe boundary behavior under zero time horizon or empty-queue immediate priority. Empirical survival regressions fit intensity parameters (`clear_rate` and cancellation attrition) separately across morning opening volatility versus stable afternoon sessions. Accounting cleanly for queue position priority shifts caused by aggressive order cancellations ahead of us prevents static arrival models from severely underestimating near-term execution hazards—while clamping malformed over-100% cancel fractions protects solver math against invalid user inputs cleanly. Cross-validating Poisson arrival survival curves against empirical realized L3 fill timestamps proves whether assumed queue attrition rates generalize across different tick widths and spread regimes. Production verification tests exercise boundary horizons (`dt -> 0`), zero volume ahead (`100%` immediate priority), and extreme cancel saturation bounds cleanly.",
