          "Inspecting `add_order(order_ref, price, qty)` and `cancel_order(order_ref)` shows clean multi-index maintenance. Adding an order inserts metadata into `self.orders`, lazily initializes `self.level_queues[price]` if empty, and appends `order_ref` to the tail. Canceling locates `price` via `self.orders[order_ref]`, deletes the order entry, and removes that exact ID from `self.level_queues[price]`.",
          "Calculating live priority inside `get_queue_ahead(my_order_ref)` guards missing IDs first (`if my_order_ref not in self.orders: return -1`), retrieves the order's `price` queue, and iterates chronologically from `index 0`, summing `self.orders[ref]['qty']` until `ref == my_order_ref` triggers `break`. Trailing orders appended later never pollute our ahead tally.",
          "Observing the printed trace demonstrates why Level-3 tracking unlocks active order retention. Sequential inserts add `1001` (`500` shares), `1002` (`1000` shares), and our ticket `1003` (`200` shares) at `100.0`, placing `1,500` shares ahead. Once `1002` cancels away, `get_queue_ahead(1003)` drops instantaneously from `1,500` down to `500`—promoting queue value immediately.",
          "Analyzing algorithmic complexity reveals one critical engineering optimization required for high-throughput matching books: in Python lists, `list.remove(order_ref)` and linear summation in `get_queue_ahead` run in `O(K)` time where `K` equals orders resting at that price level. C++ production engines replace Python lists with doubly-linked lists plus Fenwick/order-statistic trees so cancels drop to strict `O(1)` and ahead-queries stay `O(log K)` even with 5,000 resting quotes per tick. Maintaining real-time ahead-volume counts in `L3QueueTracker` turns raw exchange ITCH event streams (`add_order`, `cancel_order`, `get_queue_ahead`) into instantaneous priority telemetry (`1500 -> 500 ahead after order 1002 cancels`). Recording each resting ticket alongside per-level arrival timestamps enables deterministic accounting on every front-of-queue cancel, replace, or partial fill without scanning thousands of sibling orders per tick. Understanding exchange priority reset semantics proves vital across venues: while reducing share count keeps existing queue rank intact on Nasdaq ITCH, increasing order size or changing limit price immediately loses time priority and shunts an order straight to the tail of the level! Rigorous historical replay tests assert bit-for-bit equality between tracked `ahead_volume` counts and independent full-level scans after millions of add/cancel/execute events, test clean idempotency if duplicate network packets arrive, verify behavior on unknown order ID cancels cleanly, and confirm sub-millisecond tail timing across crowded 5,000-order price levels. Separating hash-indexed ticket metadata from level-aggregated queue sums cuts replay reconstruction time drastically across multi-million-message daily OPRA and Nasdaq ITCH archives. Whenever an exchange broadcasts partial fill, cancel, or price-modify packets out of order on redundant multicast lines, exact ticket tracking detects redundant duplicates cleanly without corrupting cumulative depth counts resting ahead of active resting firm quotes (`1500 -> 500`). Notice also exact time-priority exchange rules: canceling partial quantity keeps remaining FIFO rank preserved cleanly, whereas increasing size or changing limit price immediately resets order timestamp behind tail depth! Exhaustive replay test assertions confirm bit-for-bit equality against naive linear queue scans after randomized burst events.",
          "Kernel-based realized volatility estimators apply Parzen or Tukey-Hanning weights alongside reconstructed MBO queues to separate genuine liquidity shocks from transient quote-stuffing chatter. Adding partial-fill reduction support (`reduce_order(ref, fill_qty)`) and checking `if not self.level_queues[price]: del self.level_queues[price]` prevents empty dead price keys from leaking memory across long multi-day sessions."
