        "title": "Ornstein-Uhlenbeck Mean Reversion Calibration & Half-Life Estimation — Core Algorithm & State Representation",
        "say": [
          "To apply the continuous Ornstein-Uhlenbeck model to discrete daily or intraday bar series, quantitative desks translate differential equations into discrete autoregressive regressions. Because market prices arrive on discrete schedules rather than continuous instants, exact Euler and integrated AR(1) mappings connect statistical regressions to stochastic physics.",
          "Applying discrete time-step discretization (`dt`) turns continuous OU dynamics into a clean first-order linear autoregression: `X_t = a + b * X_{t-1} + epsilon_t`. Here intercept `a`, slope `b`, and residual variance estimate smoothly from standard ordinary least squares over historical spread observations.",
          "Stepping through `OUCalibrator.calibrate_from_ar1(a_intercept, b_slope, residual_variance, dt=1.0)`, Stationarity imposes an immediate non-negotiable invariant: slope `b_slope` must lie strictly inside `(0, 1)`. When `b_slope >= 1.0`, the spread behaves like an explosive or unit-root random walk (`NON_STATIONARY_SERIES`) and rejects calibration immediately.",
          "Inside the valid stationary domain `0 < b_slope < 1`, exact algebraic inversion recovers every continuous OU parameter: speed `theta = -math.log(b_slope) / dt`, equilibrium center `mu = a_intercept / (1.0 - b_slope)`, and expected trading half-life `half_life = math.log(2.0) / theta`.",
          "Notice the intuitive geometric meaning of half-life (`ln(2) / theta`): it measures the exact number of time periods required for half of an arbitrary price dislocation `(X_0 - mu)` to evaporate back toward equilibrium. Knowing half-life converts abstract `theta` rates directly into concrete capital-holding timelines.",
          "Sample AR(1) calibration output with `a=15.0`, `b=0.85`, `residual_var=0.40`, and `dt=1.0`: `theta` resolves to `0.1625`, `mu` resolves cleanly to `15.0 / 0.15 = 100.0`, and expected spread half-life equals `4.27 trading days` (`ln(2) / 0.1625`).",
          "Why compute half-life instead of guessing an arbitrary 20-day Bollinger moving average window? Using calibrated half-life tunes rolling Z-score lookbacks directly to each pair's natural physics, avoiding lagging averages on fast pairs and premature stops on genuinely slow structural spreads. Mapping empirical autoregressive estimates directly onto continuous-time OU parameters inside `OUCalibrator.calibrate_from_ar1` (`theta=0.1625`, `valid=True`) exposes fragile curve fits immediately when regression slope `b_slope` approaches or exceeds unity. Because half-life grows asymptotically like `ln(2) / -ln(b)`, tiny estimation errors near `b=0.98` swing implied holding horizons wildly from three weeks out to several months! Equally subtle is sample estimation bias in discrete AR(1) regressions (Nickell and Kendall small-sample downward slope bias), which makes noisy 60-day spreads look falsely faster-reverting in backtests than they behave live out of sample! Quantitative researchers therefore pair AR(1) formulas (`theta = -ln(b) / dt` and `mu = a / (1 - b)`) with sliding out-of-sample walk-forward windows, reject negative or non-stationary unit-root slopes (`b <= 0` or `b >= 1.0`) aggressively before dividing by `(1 - b)` or taking undefined logarithms, verify exact scale conversion between daily (`dt=1.0`) and intraday bar frequencies, and archive historical half-life drift across sectors so structural breaks surface rapidly. Equally valuable in live calibration infrastructure is computing confidence bounds alongside point estimates of `theta` and half-life: wide standard errors around sparse 30-day AR(1) regressions immediately alert researchers when apparent mean reversion is merely random sampling noise rather than structural equilibrium! Guarding extreme near-unit-root regressions (`b >= 1.0` or `b <= 0.0`) avoids dividing by zero cleanly during turbulent macro transitions while tracking historical slope stability cleanly. Notice also how rolling calibration windows require careful sample-size discipline: windows shorter than three expected half-lives suffer severe finite-sample downward AR(1) coefficient bias, tricking overly optimistic backtests into inventing fast mean reversion inside sluggish cointegrated pairs that bleed borrow charges live! Validating unit recovery across varying discrete bar spacings (`dt = 1.0` daily vs `dt = 1/390` minute bars) confirms exact mathematical invariance across trading frequencies cleanly. Automated regression scripts verify rejection of explosive (`b = 1.02`), exact unit-root (`b = 1.0`), and negative-oscillation slopes (`b = -0.2`) safely without math domain exceptions.",
          "Testing `b_slope=0.92` demonstrates how small shifts in autocorrelation alter capital efficiency dramatically: mean-reversion speed slows to `0.0834` and half-life doubles past `8.3 trading days`. Meanwhile, multi-horizon propagator models formalize price impact decay alongside these mean-reversion speeds when sizing realistic execution trajectories."
        ],
        "example": "Radioactive isotope decay where physics measures the half-life of atoms: financial spreads decay back to their baseline with identical exponential math.",
        "code": "import math\n\nclass OUCalibrator:\n    @staticmethod\n    def calibrate_from_ar1(a_intercept, b_slope, residual_variance, dt=1.0):\n        if b_slope >= 1.0 or b_slope <= 0:\n            return {\"valid\": False, \"reason\": \"NON_STATIONARY_SERIES\"}\n            \n        # Continuous parameters from discrete AR(1)\n        theta = -math.log(b_slope) / dt\n        mu = a_intercept / (1.0 - b_slope)\n        half_life = math.log(2.0) / theta\n        \n        sigma_sq = residual_variance * (-2.0 * math.log(b_slope)) / (dt * (1.0 - (b_slope ** 2)))\n        sigma = math.sqrt(max(0.0, sigma_sq))\n        \n        return {\n            \"valid\": True,\n            \"theta_speed\": round(theta, 4),\n            \"mu_equilibrium\": round(mu, 4),\n            \"half_life_days\": round(half_life, 2),\n            \"continuous_sigma\": round(sigma, 4)\n        }\n\n# Simulated discrete AR(1) calibration on equity spread: a = 15.0, b = 0.85 (dt = 1 day)\ncalib = OUCalibrator.calibrate_from_ar1(15.0, 0.85, 0.64, dt=1.0)\nprint(\"calibration valid:\", calib[\"valid\"])\nprint(\"mean reversion speed (theta):\", calib[\"theta_speed\"])\nprint(\"equilibrium mean (mu): $\", calib[\"mu_equilibrium\"])\nprint(\"half-life of spread:\", calib[\"half_life_days\"], \"trading days\")",
