          "Large-scale statistical arbitrage engines scan tens of thousands of candidate equity pairs nightly, evaluating stationarity slopes, holding half-lives, and standardized spread deviations across entire indices. Separating genuinely profitable opportunities from noise-dominated or agonizingly slow pairs requires strict programmatic filtering.",
          "Not all asset prices exhibit usable stationarity or viable mean-reversion turnover. Even when a statistical test confirms positive `theta`, extreme half-life horizons render trading impractical: ultra-slow reversion ties up margin capital for quarters, while hyper-fast intraday noise gets consumed completely by bid-ask spread crossing fees.",
          "`RollingZScoreFilter.evaluate_pair_tradability` filters candidate pairs along two speed guardrails before computing actionable signals. If `b_slope >= 0.98` or `half_life_days > 45.0`, it drops the candidate with `TOO_SLOW_OR_NON_STATIONARY`. If `half_life_days < 0.2` (under ~1.5 trading hours), it rejects `TOO_FAST_SLIPPAGE_DOMINATED`.",
          "Pairs clearing both speed bounds advance to standardized dislocation scoring: `z_score = round((current_spread - mu) / max(0.01, sigma_eq), 2)`. Guarding `max(0.01, sigma_eq)` protects cleanly against degenerate constant spreads during stock halts or corporate buyout pins where equilibrium dispersion vanishes.",
          "Standardized Z-scores normalize absolute price deviations into dimensionless standard deviations around equilibrium `mu`, mapping directly to clean trade signals. Reaching `z_score >= 2.0` signals overstretched positive dislocation (`ENTER_SHORT_SPREAD`); dropping to `z_score <= -2.0` flags stretched negative dislocation (`ENTER_LONG_SPREAD`).",
          "When dislocation remains small inside `|z_score| <= 0.25`, any open trade has captured equilibrium reversion and receives `EXIT_TAKE_PROFIT`, while intermediate values (`0.25 < |z_score| < 2.0`) issue `HOLD_WAIT` so existing positions avoid excessive round-trip commissions chasing sub-sigma chatter.",
          "Compare the two printed pairs: a valid pair (`b=0.88`, `half_life=5.4d`, `spread=104.5`, `mu=100.0`, `sigma=2.0`) computes `z_score = 2.25` and fires `ENTER_SHORT_SPREAD`, while a sluggish pair (`b=0.99`, `half_life=69.0d`) gets rejected instantly as `TOO_SLOW_OR_NON_STATIONARY`. Enforcing dual speed and signal strength conditions in `RollingZScoreFilter.evaluate_pair_tradability` (`ENTER_SHORT_SPREAD` at `Z=2.25, half-life=5.4d` vs `TOO_SLOW_OR_NON_STATIONARY`) keeps scarce balance-sheet capital rotating rapidly through liquid spread cycles. Holding dead-money spreads whose half-life spans sixty-nine trading days locks up prime-broker margin capacity while incurring cumulative short-stock borrow costs, financing drag, and ex-dividend cash adjustments that eat nominal spread convergence! Conversely, filtering simultaneously for bounded half-life (`<= max_half_life`) and extreme entry magnitude (`|Z| >= 2.0`) ensures only brisk, economically scalable dislocations trigger capital commitments. Portfolio scanners also maintain rolling lookback consistency proportional to half-life—sizing mean and standard-deviation buffers around two to three half-lives avoids stale long-window inertia while avoiding reactive tiny-window overfitting! Production unit tests check symmetric triggers across deep negative Z-scores (`ENTER_LONG_SPREAD`), verify safe zero-variance sigma guards against division crashes on flat spreads, confirm immediate rejection when unit-root slopes produce missing half-life bounds, and enforce priority sorting across competing sector pairs. Pairing signal entry Z-scores strictly alongside maximum tolerable half-life ceilings ensures portfolio capital avoids getting trapped in glacial macro spreads taking months to revert while accruing short borrow expenses daily. Testing symmetric long and short triggers (`|z| >= 2.0`), zero-sigma flat spread protection, and fast rejection of sluggish candidates (`b=0.99`, `half_life=69.0d`) guarantees clean risk-adjusted signal filtering. Remember why economic opportunity cost matters just as heavily as pure statistical sign flips: locking capital inside a sluggish pair yields far lower annualized return on equity than cycling liquid fast-reverting consumer or industrial spread trades every few days! Careful threshold validation guards degenerate negative variances cleanly, rejects stale NaN prices defensively, checks exact half-life cutoffs deterministically, and logs rejected pair rationales clearly across universe selection pipelines for overnight strategy curation reviews. Enforcing combined Z-score magnitude and maximum half-life ceilings stops spread strategies from holding dormant macro divergences that bleed daily short borrow fees. Filtering out slow candidates (`half_life=69.0d`) frees buying power rapidly for responsive cointegrated pairs delivering faster mean-reversion turnover. Consistent boundary validation prevents NaN variance spikes from emitting noisy entry triggers.",
          "Testing `current_spread=95.0` on the healthy pair yields `z_score=-2.50` and triggers `ENTER_LONG_SPREAD`. Across large baskets, vectorized execution algorithms invert cross-impact covariance matrices alongside these half-life filters to allocate capital cleanly without drowning edge in correlated slippage."
