          "Crucial caveat for production use: always apply Engle-Granger to continuous dividend-adjusted prices or log-prices rather than raw returns. Taking first differences strips away long-run level offsets, destroying the equilibrium gap signal you sought to capture. Two-stage estimation inside `EngleGrangerTwoStep` (`beta=1.5027`, `COINTEGRATED_PAIR_CERTIFIED`, `t-stat=-4.15 < -3.37`) keeps hedge calibration grounded in strict asymptotic distribution theory. Using tighter MacKinnon rejection thresholds (`-3.37`) instead of naive standard normal `-1.96` or univariate Dickey-Fuller tables properly accounts for the extra parameter degree of freedom consumed when estimating hedge ratio `beta` via OLS on the first pass! Skipping that stricter MacKinnon cutoff floods candidate universes with phantom false-positive pairs that unwind painfully during live trading regimes. Production stat-arb pipelines also guard reverse-regression asymmetry closely: because regressing `Y ~ X` can occasionally yield weaker or stronger residual t-statistics than `X ~ Y` when asset volatilities differ sharply, automated calibration desks inspect both orientations alongside zero-variance denominator guards before locking active pair definitions! Rigorous validation suites test synthetic random walks confirming high rejection power, verify exact OLS beta recovery (`1.5027` near true `1.5`), handle short or flat series safely without crashes, and integrate multi-asset Johansen trace checks whenever hedging baskets exceed two instruments. Checking Dickey-Fuller t-statistics rigorously against MacKinnon sample-adjusted thresholds (`-4.15 < -3.37`) eliminates naive false discoveries caused by estimating cointegrating slope `beta` inside finite historical windows. Unit tests verify both synthetic cointegrated recovery (`beta=1.5027`) and un-cointegrated random-walk rejection power cleanly across varying lag lengths and zero-variance outlier protections. Inspecting reverse regressions (`X ~ Y` versus `Y ~ X`) alongside zero-mean spread residuals catches scaling instabilities early when one pair stock sits five times more volatile than its sibling leg! Beyond single-pair ADF statistics, multi-leg sector ETF arbitrage baskets extend naturally toward Johansen trace and maximum-eigenvalue cointegration tests, identifying stationary linear combinations invariant to arbitrary base ticker ordering. Archiving nightly OLS beta residuals cleanly across rolling 120-day and 252-day windows further reveals gradual drift cleanly well ahead of sudden correlation breakdown events. Separating OLS parameter fitting from strict MacKinnon hypothesis validation trains statistical engines never to confuse in-sample residual tightening for out-of-sample stationarity. Inspecting residual stability across reverse regressions further uncovers asymmetric liquidity betas well before live hedging execution.",
