          "Option pricing theory assumes constant annualized volatility `sigma` across strikes and expirations, making `sigma` the sole unobservable market input in the analytical pricing formula. Yet real option exchanges never quote volatility directly—markets broadcast executable bid and ask dollar premiums.",
