          "Because root solvers like Newton-Raphson depend heavily on clean initial starting guesses to converge rapidly without wandering into flat tails where Vega vanishes, options desks bootstrap iterations with the famous `Brenner-Subrahmanyam` closed-form approximation inside `IVSurfaceFoundations.brenner_subrahmanyam_seed(c_atm, s, t)`.",
          "Examine the exact closed-form approximation derived from linearizing normal cumulative distribution near ATM (`d1 ~= 0.5 * sigma * sqrt(T)`): `seed = sqrt(2 * pi / t) * (c_atm / s)`. Guarding `t <= 0 or s <= 0` returns sensible `0.20` baseline fallback safely.",
