          "Extreme-value null checking confirms `expected_max_sharpe(1000)` hits extreme-value null benchmark near `3.87`—shattering any naive illusion that a `2.0` Sharpe backtest proves genuine edge without adjusting for trial count. Quantifying multiple-testing selection inflation cleanly inside `DSRFoundations.expected_max_sharpe` (`1 trial -> 0.0`, `10 trials -> 2.415`, climbing near `3.87` at `1000` trials) exposes quantitative finance's pervasive publication and p-hacking bias! Suppose an analyst tests one thousand completely random, zero-edge technical indicator rules over historical data and cherry-picks strictly the luckiest backtest winner: by Gaussian extreme-value order statistics (`sqrt(2 * ln(N))` adjusted by Euler-Mascheroni correction), the expected maximum sample Sharpe of pure zero-alpha coin flips climbs nearly to `3.9`! Presenting that `2.5` or `3.0` backtest Sharpe to an investment committee without adjusting hurdle rate `SR_0` for trial count `N` mistakes random data-mining noise for genuine market alpha! Computing exact asymptotic false-discovery null expectations establishes an objective statistical bar every quantitative strategy must clear—scaling gracefully from zero inflation (`0.0`) on single pre-registered hypotheses up to steep institutional hurdles across massive grid searches while handling trial variance edge inputs safely. Selection bias from multiple hypothesis testing silently invalidates naive Sharpe ratios; `DeflatedSharpeFoundations` computes expected maximum false-discovery Sharpe across `N` independent strategy trials (`1->0.0, 10->2.415`). Even purely random zero-mean returns appear remarkably profitable when researchers run hundreds of backtests and publish only the top-ranked equity curve. Implementing False Discovery Rate expectations using Bailey and Lopez de Prado Euler-Mascheroni Gaussian extreme-value approximations quantifies exact inflated hurdle rates dynamically as trial counts climb. Comparing single-trial zero penalties (`0.0`) directly against ten-trial (`2.415`) and five-hundred-trial (`3.0+`) thresholds gives quantitative researchers visceral intuition around data-mining danger. Closed-form analytical expectations run instantaneously inside experiment trackers before full statistical significance testing executes. Quantifying multiple-testing selection bias directly via Euler-Mascheroni Gaussian extreme-value hurdles (`10->2.415`) reveals immediately how large backtest searches turn pure noise into illusory high-Sharpe curves.",
